Wait. E_C2 = set of proper future C2 metric extensions. E_C0 = set of proper future C0 extensions. Since every C2 extension is a C0 extension, E_C2 ⊆ E_C0. So E_C2 is the *smaller* class of extensions. Inextendibility in class X = "E_X is empty". 

- "no proper future C2 extension" means E_C2 = ∅. Since E_C2 ⊆ E_C0, E_C2 = ∅ does NOT imply E_C0 = ∅. So C2-inextendibility is *weaker* than C0-inextendibility. Correct: C0-inextendibility (E_C0 = ∅) implies E_C2 = ∅ (C2-inextendibility). So C0 is stronger.
- The forbidden transfer is from "no C2 extension" to "this class" (C0) — forbidden because C2-inextendibility doesn't give C0-inextendibility.

